in neovim with lazyvim, how do i make it so that bg-red-700 will be treated as one word
-----
You need to configure Neovim to treat hyphens as part of word characters. Add this to your LazyVim config:

```lua
vim.opt.iskeyword:append("-")
```

This modifies the `iskeyword` option to include hyphens, so `bg-red-700` will be treated as a single word for operations like `w`, `b`, `*`, `ciw`, etc.

You can add this to your `~/.config/nvim/lua/config/options.lua` file in LazyVim.
-------
hmm i guess i can also just get used to doing dW instead of dw
-----
Yes, exactly. `dW` uses WORD motion which treats whitespace-separated sequences as single units, so `bg-red-700` would be deleted entirely with `dW`.

The difference:
- `dw` - deletes a word (stops at hyphens, dots, etc.)
- `dW` - deletes a WORD (only stops at whitespace)

Same applies to other motions: `cW`, `yW`, `*W` isn't a thing but `g*` works on partial matches. Using `dW`/`cW` is often more practical for code with hyphens, dots, or other punctuation.
-------
i added some keymaps to my keymaps.lua. How can i make nvim refresh? i dont wanna quit out of my real project
-----
You can reload your keymaps configuration with:

```
:luafile ~/.config/nvim/lua/config/keymaps.lua
```

Or if you're in LazyVim, you can use:

```
:source ~/.config/nvim/lua/config/keymaps.lua
```

Alternatively, restart just the Lua state without closing buffers:

```
:LspRestart
:lua package.loaded['config.keymaps'] = nil
:lua require('config.keymaps')
```

The first approach is usually the simplest for reloading keymaps.
-------
